<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">1992 Summer Olympics</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Barcelona 1992</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 1992 Summer Olympics were held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, from 25 July to 9 August 1992. The first Games after the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> without a major boycott, they included a Unified Team of former Soviet republics and a reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> returned after decades of exclusion. The preparations transformed the city's waterfront and transport, and became a model for urban renewal through the Olympics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/1992-summer-olympics.jpg" alt="1992 Summer Olympics" width="330" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nikolai Karaneschev · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barcelona_AUGUST_1992_the_Olympic_Games_(Juegos_Olímpicos_de_Barcelona_1992)_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>On 17 October 1986 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> was chosen to host the Games at the 91st IOC Session in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. It won over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, Birmingham, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brisbane">Brisbane</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, with a majority of 47 votes from 85 of the 89 IOC members voting by secret ballot. Barcelona was the second-largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the capital of Catalonia, and the hometown of the then IOC president Juan Antonio Samaranch, who abstained. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a> had announced a bid but withdrew in March 1986. Beginning in 1994 the IOC decided to hold the Summer and Winter Games in alternating even-numbered years, so 1992 was the last year both were staged together.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>At the opening ceremony the cauldron was lit by the Paralympic archer Antonio Rebollo, who shot a flaming arrow over its top to ignite the gas rising from it, a method designed so that a miss could not land the arrow in the stadium. A recording of the song <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, by Freddie Mercury and Mike Moran, played at the start; Mercury had died eight months earlier. Amigos Para Siempre, by Andrew Lloyd Webber and Don Black, was sung at the closing ceremony by Sarah Brightman and José Carreras. The official mascot was Cobi, a Catalan sheepdog in cubist style designed by Javier Mariscal.</p><p>The programme had 257 events in 25 sports, with Basque pelota, roller hockey and taekwondo as demonstration sports. A total of 169 nations sent 9,356 athletes. Twelve of the former Soviet republics formed a Unified Team, which topped the medal table with 45 gold and 112 medals in all. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> sent their own teams for the first time since 1936, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> for the first time since 1928. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> competed as one team after reunification in 1990, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> returned after a 32-year ban over apartheid. The Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> was barred by UN sanctions, though individual athletes took part as Independent Olympic Participants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Games were the first since the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> and the first unaffected by boycotts since 1972. In the women's 10,000 metres, the Ethiopian winner Derartu Tulu and the South African Elana Meyer ran a victory lap hand in hand. Before the Games, between 29 June and 14 July 1992, Spanish police arrested 45 Catalan pro-independence activists, journalists and politicians in Operation Garzón; political activists argue it was used to weaken the left-wing independence movement. ETA offered a truce on 10 July, which the Spanish government rejected, and no attack came during the Games.</p><p>The Games changed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. The Olympic Village and Olympic Port at Poblenou opened the city to the sea, ring roads were built, and El Prat airport gained two new terminals.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “1992 Summer Olympics” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sanctions also cut the country out of sport: it missed the <b>1992 Summer Olympics</b> and was excluded from international football from 1992 to 1996, though its basketball team won the EuroBasket in 1995, 1997 and 2001 and the world titles of 1998 and 2002.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>At the <b>1992 Summer Olympics</b> in Barcelona the South African team used &quot;Ode to Joy&quot; and a neutral flag instead.</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-stem-van-suid-afrika">Die Stem van Suid-Afrika</a></li><li><blockquote>The 1992 <b>Barcelona Olympics</b> and Seville Expo marked modernisation.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-spain">Kingdom of Spain</a></li></ul></section>
